- How would latency constraints affect routing decisions?
- How would you add confidence scores to intent classification?
- What testing strategy would you use for a keyword-based classifier?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design modular, policy-driven prompt systems that scale across tenants, balance latency with safety, and provide auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt blocks
- Policy graph
- Per-tenant constraints
- Latency-aware composition
- Determinism and reproducibility
- Provenance and audit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch (pseudo-implementation)
function composePrompt(intent, blockLibrary, policy){
  const allowed = blockLibrary.filter(b => policy.allows(b.tag, intent));
  const prompt = allowed.map(b => b.template).join("\n");
  const audit = { intent, blocks: allowed.map(b => b.id), provenance: policy.provenance };
  return { prompt, audit };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate determinism when blocks or transformations are nondeterministic?
- How would policy graph evolution affect audit integrity and prompt reproducibility?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to enforce data policies in prompt processing, not just theory. It probes edge-case handling and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt safety layering
- Data_sensitivity tagging
- Policy-driven guard rails and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal guard example
function guardPrompt(prompt, policy){
  const intent = parseIntent(prompt);
  const data = tagSensitivity(prompt, policy);
  if(!allowed(data, policy)){ throw new Error('Policy violation'); }
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  const check = `compliant:${Boolean(guarded)}`;
  return {guardedPrompt: guarded, check};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model conflicting signals (privacy vs analytics) and resolve them?
- How would you test for prompt leakage across multi-hop prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to enforce tenancy isolation and data minimization in prompts and outputs, plus post-checks before delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented prompts, tenancy scoping, redaction, post-filtering, test harness concepts.

## Code Example
```javascript
function wrapPrompt(tenantId, userPrompt) {
  // basic tenant-scoped redaction and prefix
  const safePrompt = `[TENANT:${tenantId}] PROMPT: ${userPrompt}`;
  return safePrompt;
}

function mockLLM(prompt){
  // simulate a model that may leak data
  return `Response for ${prompt} with tenant id ${prompt.match(/TENANT:(\\w+)/)[1]}`;
}

function postCheck(response, tenantId){
  // naive leakage check
  if (response.includes(tenantId)) return null;
  return response;
}

console.log(postCheck(mockLLM(wrapPrompt('T123','List logs for user emails')),
  'T123'));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve this for real-world tenancy boundaries and performance at scale?
- What tests would you add to catch redaction misses and leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic redaction rules per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a modular, latency-aware prompt routing flow in an edge/cloud context. It requires turning a user goal into a module selection, a concise execution plan, and a concrete payload while considering safety and edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing and modular design
- PII redaction and latency constraints
- Edge-case handling and fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router sketch
function routePrompt(req){
  const intent = req.intent||'';
  const data = req.data_sensitivity||'';
  const latency = req.latency_budget||Infinity;
  if(intent.includes('summary') && data==='PII' && latency<=1000){
    return {module:'Summary-Builder', plan:['fetch','redact','summarize'], payload:{regions:req.regions||['us-east','us-west'], redact:true}};
  }
  return {module:'Data-Extractor', plan:['extract'], payload:{}};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this for latency budgets and safety guards?
- How would you extend to handle parallel modules and partial results?
